\title{What is open is a \code{\verb~|sorry~}; proofs are by analysis, for one plan; kernels return \code{\verb~|Option~}}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{architecture}
\tag{process}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted. Decided by the maintainer on 2026-11-24, after the state of the proof had been reported in a way that hid what was missing.}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Open obligations are \code{\verb~|sorry~}.} The main theorem had been stated with the unproved ranges as a hypothesis, \code{\verb~|ExpfMainRangesChecked~}, and every report said that it had no \code{\verb~|sorry~} and only the standard axioms. That was true and said nothing: a hypothesis does not show among the axioms. An open statement is now a named lemma that ends in \code{\verb~|sorry~}, and the theorem that uses it is stated without the hypothesis, so that \code{\verb~|sorryAx~} shows.}
\li{\strong{No enumeration.} A proof about a function does not run the function on each input of a range, in the kernel or by compiled code, and the repository is not organised around doing so. The certificate sets, \code{\verb~|scripts/exhaustive.py~}, and the lemmas that assembled correct rounding from checked blocks are removed. This goes further than [dec-K2Q4](dec-K2Q4), which it supersedes on this point. \code{\verb~|checkrange~} and the oracles remain as tests.}
\li{\strong{One plan.} Proofs are kept for \code{\verb~|double_eval~} only. The proofs of the short paths of \code{\verb~|double_eval_fma~}, \code{\verb~|static_rounding~} and \code{\verb~|integer_eval~}, about 500 lines from \pr{17}, are removed; they are in the history.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|Option~}.} An extracted function returns \code{\verb~|Option~}, and \code{\verb~|none~} is an execution that reaches an operation C++ leaves undefined. The alternative, a total function with a separate predicate for when it is defined, was set aside: it needs two generated definitions that must agree, and a theorem can forget the predicate.}
}
